For every experimental ignition point the **same 0-D constant-volume solver** is
used as for the parent reference; measured and computed delays use different
criteria (OH*/CH d/dt_max in the shock tube vs max dT/dt here), so the
comparison is a diagnostic of model error, not a like-for-like measurement.

### 2.4 Reduction strategies compared

1. **DRGEP** (Pepiot-Desjardins & Pitsch): `r_AB = |Σ_i ν_A,i q_i δ_B,i| /
   max(Σ_i |ν_A,i q_i|, P_A)` with max-product path propagation; importance
   `ε_A = max_B R_AB` over target species (fuels + O2), aggregated over sampled
   states by **maximum** (the classical choice).
2. **Flux participation** `f_A = Σ_i |ν_A,i q_i|`, max over 2487 sampled states
   taken from the parent trajectory (22 sample times per ignition case,
   train cases only).
3. **LOO observable sensitivity**: for each removable species, build the
   mechanism without it and record the largest relative observable change over
   a case sub-set.  This targets exactly the validated quantities.
4. **Greedy observable-driven add-back** (final design method): starting from a
   base set that is too small, repeatedly add back the single removed species
   that most reduces the worst-case *normalised* error
   `J = max(max|e_ig|/0.30, max|e_fl|/0.10)` (a hard failure adds 100).
   Two-stage search: all candidates are screened on 36 worst-case ignition
   cases, the best 6 are re-evaluated on a 126-case probe (110 ignition +
   16 flames) before acceptance.  Code: `src/greedy_search.py`.
5. **Reaction-level trimming** (`src/reaction_trim.py`): with the species set
   frozen, every reaction is screened one at a time on a cheap probe; reactions
   whose individual removal costs < 0.02 in J are removed as a batch and the
   batch is re-validated on the full probe, with bisection if the combined
   effect exceeds 0.05.

### 2.5 Acceptance tolerances (fixed before the sweep)

* ignition: max |Δτ/τ| ≤ 30 % over all non-failed cases, median ≤ 10 %;
* flame: max |Δs_L/s_L| ≤ 10 % (numerical grid uncertainty on s_L is ≈ 2 %, see §5);
* no new hard failures (cases that ignite or burn in the parent must not fail);
* element balance and thermodynamic data of retained species unchanged.

These levels are of the order of the facility-to-facility scatter of shock-tube
ignition data (≈ 20–30 %) and of laminar flame-speed measurements (≈ 5–10 %),
i.e. they are intended to be "useful accuracy" rather than "reproduce the
parent bit-for-bit".

## 3. Parent reference results

* 400/401 cases converge; the single exception (`ig_CH4_phi2_T1000_P1`,
  φ = 2 methane at 1 atm) does not ignite in the parent within the integration
  window and is excluded everywhere.
* τ (max dT/dt) vs τ_cross (ΔT = 400 K): ratio median 1.017, p5–p95
  0.906–1.139, extreme 0.244–1.218 → the two definitions agree to ≈ ±15 % for
  most cases; all error metrics in this report use the max-dT/dt definition.
* Parent wall time: ignition median 0.41 s/case, flame median 44.8 s/case
  (1488 CPU-s for the whole 401-case matrix).
* Flame speeds computed for the parent at 1 atm, 300 K, φ = 1 are physically
  sane: CH4 0.378, C3H8 0.415, n-C4H10 0.420, C2H6 0.461, C2H4 0.659,
  syngas (H2/CO = 1) 1.168, H2 2.293 m/s.  (Sanity check against widely quoted
  atmospheric values, not a downloaded data set.)
* Parent HP-equilibrium temperatures of six fuel/air mixtures are stored in
  `results/verify_all.json` and used as the thermodynamic reference in §5.

## 4. Experimental comparison

### 4.1 Set A (H2/O2 shock tubes, 188 points)

log10(τ_parent/τ_exp) over 146 usable points: **median +0.036** (parent 8 %
slow), p10 −0.260, p90 +0.387.  Per-dataset medians range from −0.285
(zenodo.4639751, 10 atm H2/O2/Ar) to +0.836 (zenodo.4639759); two propane-
nominal/H2 datasets sit at −0.27/−0.29.  Parent failures (no ignition in the
integration window) occur in 5 of the 23 datasets, all at high pressure
(13–40 atm) — these are excluded from the statistics rather than counted as
errors.

### 4.2 Set B (hydrocarbon shock tubes, 185 points after data cleaning)

This is the data set against which the reduction can actually be judged:
benzene, ethylene and methane are exactly the fuels whose intermediate chemistry
is deleted during reduction.

**Data-quality correction.**  Three archived datasets (`4733738`, `4733746`,
`7771883`) declare their ignition delays in **ms**, but the recorded numbers
(34–750 and 100–1765) sit in exactly the same numerical range as sibling
datasets from the same laboratory and identical conditions that declare **µs**
(`5702629` vs `4733746`: identical mixture AR/O2/C6H6 = 0.8701/0.1257/0.0042,
same 2.44 atm, delays 35–688 in both files).  Interpreting them literally gives
delays of 0.03–1.8 s, which contradicts both kinetic expectation and the parent
mechanism; interpreting them as µs makes `4733746` an exact duplicate of
`5702629` (13 points removed by the deduplication check) and brings the 16
archived datasets (15 distinct after the merge) into mutual consistency.  The
correction is implemented as an explicit flagged rule in `src/expt_hc.py` and
is the only data manipulation applied.

log10(τ/τ_exp) with the parent mechanism: median **−0.101**, p10 −0.326,
p90 +0.254 over 185 points; per-dataset medians lie between −0.34 and +0.19
(worst: zenodo.7772066 CH4/H2/Ar 4 atm, −0.34).  That is, on these
macroscopic hydrocarbon observables the parent is accurate to ≈ ±0.3 dex.

**Effect of the reduction on the experimental comparison** (all four
mechanisms reproduce the parent's agreement with the data):

