### 6.1 Species search in observable space (the winning method)

The ranking-based sets above are one-shot: LOO ranks the species once, a
threshold cut produces a mechanism, and the error is whatever it is.  The
greedy observable-driven add-back instead *closes the loop* on the validated
observables.  Trajectory (probe = 126 cases: 110 ignition + 16 flames):

| step | species | added | J (=max(max|e_ig|/0.30, max|e_fl|/0.10)) | ig max % | fl max % |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 63 (lut50) | — | 2.156 | 51.7 | 21.6 |
| 1 | 64 | pC3H4 | 1.520 | 24.6 | 15.2 |
| 2 | 65 | C2H3CHO | 1.131 | 21.3 | 11.3 |
| 3 | 66 | aC3H4 | **0.877** | 20.1 | 8.8 |

Evaluated afterwards on the **full 401-case matrix** (not the probe), the
66-species mechanism gives ig max **28.6 %**, ig p95 9.5 %, ig median 1.6 %,
flame max **8.8 %** and **zero** hard failures: it satisfies both acceptance
tolerances on every case.

Two local-optimality checks were then run on the full matrix:

* **adding** any of the 45 remaining parent species: the best single addition
  (HCCOH) moves J from 0.953 to 0.952 — no single species fixes the residual
  error;
* **removing** any single species: the cheapest removal (c-C4H5) pushes J to
  1.108 (ig max 33.2 %) — every species in the set is load-bearing at the
  tolerance level.

So within this sub-set construction the 66-species mechanism is a two-sided
local optimum, and **the remaining 28.6 % error on a single case (benzene/air,
φ = 2, 2000 K, 30 atm) is not attributable to one missing species**; it would
require either a multi-species aromatic sub-mechanism or rate re-fitting.

The parallel attempt to reach tolerance from a *smaller* base failed: starting
from lut40 (53 species, 12 hard failures) the same greedy needed 7 additions to
remove all failures and then stalled at J ≈ 1.59 (60 species, ig max 47.8 %,
flame max 15.9 %) — i.e. the 60-species regime is not reachable at tolerance by
this route, and the delivered 66-species set is 6 species above the best
sub-tolerance result found.

Reading of the table:

* The **ignition error is dominated by a handful of cases**: for lut50 the worst
  case is iC4H10 φ = 0.5 at 1600 K (51.7 %) while the median error is 1.98 %;
  for loo50 iC4H10 φ = 0.5 at 1000–1250 K reaches 130 %.  Benzene at φ = 2,
  T = 2000 K and iC4H10 φ = 0.5 are the recurring problem cases for every
  criterion.
* **A single high-T benzene case is the binding constraint** for 73-species sets
  (lut60: max 23.6 % on ig_C6H6_φ2_T2000_P30).
* **Compute cost falls much more slowly than the species count**: a 63-species
  mechanism is only 2.3× cheaper per ignition case and 2.7× cheaper per flame
  case than the 111-species parent (flame cases remain the expensive ones, and
  they dominate any reactor-network or CFD use).  "Smaller" and "cheaper" are
  different objectives and both are reported.
* Below ≈ 43 species the trade-off breaks down entirely: hard ignition failures
  appear (12–324 of 379 cases) and the thermodynamic floor of §5 is violated.

