### 7.1 Reaction-network reduction (activity-ranked trimming)

Reducing the *reaction* network on top of a fixed species set was attempted
three times.  The first two attempts used a per-reaction screening loop
(`src/reaction_trim.py`) and produced no usable output at a cost of ≈ 8.6 × 10^5
core-seconds (§8.6).  The approach that worked (`src/trim_flux.py`) ranks the
462 reactions of the 66-species set by their maximum normalised flux
participation over the 2487 parent trajectory samples
(`results/rxn_activity.json`), and validates a ladder of "delete the k least
active reactions" variants — each on the full 401-case matrix, with the same
parent-referenced tolerances, stopping at the first violation
(job 7958806, 4.9 × 10^4 core-s).

| reactions removed | reactions | ig max % | ig p95 % | ig med % | flame max % | flame med % | hard fails |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 (base) | 462 | 28.59 | 9.53 | 1.64 | 8.77 | 2.77 | 0 |
| 5 | 457 | 28.59 | 9.53 | 1.64 | 8.77 | 2.77 | 0 |
| 10 | 452 | 28.59 | 9.53 | 1.64 | 8.96 | 2.77 | 0 |
| 20 | 442 | 28.57 | 9.53 | 1.64 | 8.76 | 2.77 | 0 |
| 30 | 432 | 28.57 | 9.53 | 1.65 | 8.75 | 2.76 | 0 |
| 45 | 417 | 28.58 | 9.52 | 1.67 | 8.68 | 2.72 | 0 |
| 60 | 402 | 28.60 | 9.52 | 1.70 | 8.60 | 2.71 | 0 |
| 80 | 382 | 28.98 | 9.54 | 1.77 | 8.42 | 2.57 | 0 |
| 105 | 357 | 28.87 | 9.51 | 1.73 | 8.29 | 2.83 | 0 |
| **135** | **327** | **28.59** | **10.49** | **2.00** | **6.95** | **2.66** | **0** |
| 170 | 292 | 35.86 | 14.39 | 2.49 | 8.76 | 3.31 | 0 (tolerance violated) |

Reading of the ladder:

* The reaction network is **strongly over-complete for these observables**:
  deleting 135 of 462 reactions (29 %) leaves the worst-case ignition error
  unchanged (28.59 % on the same benzene case), moves the ignition median from
  1.64 % to 2.00 %, and *improves* the worst-case flame error from 8.77 % to
  6.95 % — i.e. the removed reactions were not merely redundant but slightly
  detrimental to the flame-speed agreement of this species set.
* The break is sharp in *chemistry*, not in the error metric: the 135 removed
  reactions all have individual activity ≤ **0.0217** (fraction of the largest
  reaction flux in any sampled state) whereas the 35 next-least-active
  reactions (0.023–0.045) are needed — deleting them pushes the worst ignition
  case (n-C4H10, φ = 0.5, 1000 K, 1 atm) to 35.9 % and p95 to 14.4 %.
* The removed set is 111 C2–C4 reactions, 20 aromatic (C5/C6-ring) reactions and
  4 C0–C1 reactions; **no species is orphaned** (every species still appears in
  at least one retained reaction), so the trimmed mechanism remains chemically
  connected.
* Verification of the delivered trimmed file: element imbalance 0, unbalanced
  reactions 0, thermo difference 0, max |ΔT_eq| = 0 K (`verify_trim3.json`).
* Experimental check (set B, 185 points): median log10(τ/τ_exp) = −0.118
  (parent −0.101), median |Δlog10 τ| vs parent 0.012, p95 0.036, max 0.173 —
  indistinguishable from the untrimmed 66-species set (0.010/0.035/0.176).
* Experimental flame check (set D, 70 points): mean |Δs_L/s_L| vs parent 2.16 %,
  p95 7.00 %, max 8.28 % — inside the 10 % flame tolerance on every measured
  point; the untrimmed set gives 1.39 / 7.90 / 9.28 % and the 73-species set
  1.10 / 7.42 / 8.44 %.  Trimming the reaction network therefore costs a little
  in the mean but improves the tail.

The delivered primary mechanism is therefore the **66-species / 327-reaction**
set, and the ladder above is the quantitative accuracy/reaction-count
trade-off: −29 % reactions for at most +0.4 pp of worst-case ignition error and
−1.8 pp of worst-case flame error.

## 8. Negative results, failures and limitations

1. **DRGEP is inert for this mechanism/case configuration.**  With 2487 sampled
   states, ε_A ≈ 1.0 for 105 of 111 species (only AR, N2 and C6H5C2H differ), so
   a threshold cut removes exactly one species.  Reason: the classical
   max-over-states aggregation of a max-product path metric saturates when any
   single state links a species to a target.  Ranking quality is worse than
   useless here — Spearman ρ(ε_DRGEP from a 6-case sample, LOO observable
   importance) = 0.067 (p = 0.51, n = 98) — whereas LOO-based ranking is stable
   (ρ = 0.948 between train-selected and test-selected rankings; ρ = 0.991
   between 37-case and 114-case train probes).  Conclusion: for this task the
   ranking criterion should be *defined on the validated observables*, not on
   error-propagation proxies.
2. **Sub-40-species mechanisms are physically degraded even when numerically
   "accurate".**  Element balance and thermo consistency are preserved exactly by
   construction, yet H2/air equilibrium at 27 species is 985 K instead of 2833 K
   because H2O is no longer in the species set.  Reporting such a set as a
   "successful 27-species reduction" (its ignition errors are only ~4 % median)
   would be misleading; the check in §5 is the guard.
3. **Experimental flame validation is thinner than the ignition one.**  Of the
   94 SciExpeM laminar-burning-velocity datasets, 73 contain a fuel of this
   study, but only 70 of their points are usable here (1 atm, 280–420 K, all
   species in USC-II): 53 H₂, 10 benzene and 7 CH₄ points from 7 datasets
   (§4.5).  The other points are NH₃-blended, He/Ar/CO₂-diluted, or at elevated
   pressure/temperature.  The H₂ points carry little reduction information
   (§4.3), so the *discriminating* experimental flame evidence rests on 17
   points from 2 fuels — thin, and a limitation of the public data, not of the
   method.  Reduction error itself is additionally quantified numerically on 21
   parent flame cases (§6).
4. **The H2/O2 experimental set cannot discriminate the reduction** (§4.3).
5. **No rate re-optimisation was performed.**  All reduced mechanisms are
   sub-sets; errors are therefore a lower bound on what a re-fitted mechanism of
   the same size could achieve, and an upper bound on the "purity" of the
   sub-set approach.
6. **Reaction-level trimming: two failures before a working strategy.**  The
   first attempt (job 7956769) kept one `multiprocessing` pool alive over ~460
   candidate mechanisms; every worker accumulated a Cantera `Solution` per
   candidate, the run thrashed and was killed at its wall limit — 4.37 × 10^5
   core-seconds charged, no usable output (only the log survived).  The second
   (job 7957806) used bounded batches (10 candidates per pool) and unbuffered
   logging, but each of the 462 candidates still had to be integrated on a
   20-case probe containing the slowest low-temperature conditions (~45 core-s
   per case evaluation, ≈ 9.2 × 10^3 evaluations); it was killed when its
   allocation was exhausted (4.18 × 10^5 core-s charged, outputs
   `UNAVAILABLE`).  The third attempt abandoned per-reaction screening entirely
   and validated only ~11 cumulative activity-ranked variants on the full
   matrix (job 7958806, 4.9 × 10^4 core-s, 12 min wall) and produced the
   result in §7.1.  The two failed jobs cost ≈ 8.5 × 10^5 core-seconds, i.e.
   ≈ 65 % of all remote CPU charged in this run, with no scientific output;
   they are reported because that is part of the honest accounting of an
   exploratory run.  Recorded lesson: when each observable evaluation is
   expensive, screen candidate *reductions* on the aggregate validated metric
   (a ladder), not candidate *reactions* on a probe.
7. Unfinished side experiment: a grid/Soret sensitivity study of the parent
   flame speed (`src/flame_numcheck.py`) was started locally twice and stopped
   for budget reasons; no results file was produced.  Flame-flame comparisons in
   this report always use the same settings for parent and reduced mechanisms,
   so the reduction error is unaffected by that incomplete study.

